  "result": "`summarize()` now takes about 30 ms for 200,000 orders, down from minutes, and its output is unchanged. `npm test` passes, 4 of 4.\n\nThere were three causes of the slowness, all in `src/report.js`:\n- **Duplicate check:** `seen.includes` scanned an array for every order. It is now a `Set`.\n- **Customer lookup:** `groups.find` scanned every customer for every order. It is now a `Map` from customer id to group.\n- **Sorting:** the leaderboard was re-sorted after every order. It is now sorted once at the end. The ranking order always ends up unique, because `byRank` falls back to the customer id and each customer appears once. So one final sort gives the same order as sorting every time.\n\nI added two tests to `test/report.test.js`:\n- **Equivalence test:** it runs 3,000 generated orders through a copy of the original algorithm and compares the results with `deepStrictEqual` at limits 1, 10 and 100. The data includes duplicate ids, refunded and pending orders, EUR amounts and ties on spend and first-order time.\n- **Speed test:** it fails if 200,000 orders take 1 second or more.\n\nThe equivalence check differs from the original in one place: a customer id of `NaN`. The old `find` never matched it, so it created a new group each time, while the `Map` does match it. I left that alone because customer ids shouldn't be `NaN`.",
